, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"id" : "obj-48",
					"linecount" : 11,
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 17.0, 16.0, 834.0, 158.0 ],
					"text" : "This is an example of how you can use the D4 external. It is, by default, contained inside the D4.calc~ abstraction. D4 and D4.calc~ require the supporting abstractions (highlighted in red below, with blue representing the bare minimum necessary to get you going). To test, turn on the ezdac~ and slowly bring up the main output level. Now you can explore one of the settings found on top by toggling different behaviors (one at a time), or manually adjust values above the D4.calc~. The best way to observe the output is to use D4.meter.monitor.cube~ to monitor signal. Use D4.timeline editor to edit the spatial parameters via GUI, and D4.mask.editor to edit and D4.mask.renderer to render signal's mask. Use Preset 2 below to observe source with a modified radius. To edit the mask, select the Preset 3 (it will increase radius to 360) and set the D4.mask.editor bus to \"test\" which is this D4.calc~'s context or bus, and edit its mask by clicking and drawing on the mask editor window. See CONTROLS subpatch inside D4.mask.editor for additional info on the mask editor's controls. Next, try the D4.3D.visualizer~ by setting its bus to \"test\". Finally, don't forget to hover over nlets for hints.\n\nPlease note optional second creation argument that specifies the abstraction D4~ output generator will use for per-channel processing. This is particularly\nuseful in sonification scenarios where you may want to apply mask to parameters other than amplitude"
				}

			}
